recommendations.text:
Keep Delay Tactics off. They cannot pass this round under any value. In a later round, poaching could come to the Board only if every condition holds:
- Boeing's fps is on the public record and not in service, and NGSA is in development;
- no earlier Delay Tactics move has been made;
- the move gains at least $1B in the expected column;
- the CEO records setting aside the team's stricter condition;
- the public statement is silent on it.
On this grid one move never earns more than +0.29 $B, so the Board expects it to stay off.

Guard the thin peak-cash headroom. Keep the Alt plan's $0.75B budgeted from 2037. The CFO should re-test the $5.2B line each budget cycle through the Audit Committee. Bring back to the Board, as a matter of abnormal risk, any of the following:
- an NGSA bill above about $36.4B (+$0.88B);
- any Alt spend falling before 2037;
- a rate below about $1.07 per EUR (NGSA alone) or $1.09 per EUR (with early Alt spend).
The record gives no FCF path for 2030-36, so present the path with the first budget.

Prepare the 2035 gate now, with one programme at a time. On current bills a 2035 A350 Re-engine overlaps NGSA's last two development years and peaks at about $6.67B a year in 2035-36 against the $5.2B line. The Board would veto it as the added item. Bring the 2035 grid with:
- the overlap, the strain and peak cash;
- the NGSA schedule actually reached;
- any Boeing 787 Re-engine on the record.
The A350 Re-engine stays on hold until NGSA development spend rolls off.

cfo.memo:
We've always said safety, then the balance sheet.

UltraFan NB launch_if_selected: I co-sign. Against hold it is 0.00 in six columns, and +10.66, +12.46 or +26.17 where we are selected. On the CEO's weights, which I confirm at 0.10 for selection, it is +1.43 expected: +5.48 on uniform weights, +2.86 at 0.20. Its worst column is 0.00 against an $8B bill. If it triggers, it costs about £0.84bn a year for seven years: 22-23% of FY2026 free cash flow guidance. It is funded from cash, with no equity, no borrowing and leverage unchanged. It is redeployed inside a flat R&D envelope, not added.

Plainly: a triggered Solo commits $8B against the $4B bounded-risk line. I sign that only on a selection. Holding the line strictly would cost the whole option, $1.43B, and that premium is the CEO's call. Plan for failure: an airframer can change its code before 2037. A Solo cancelled in 2035 writes off $5.71B, inside its bill.

I withhold my signature on four items:
- an unconditional Solo: -4.44 expected and -8.00 in four columns;
- a Joint Venture commit before P&W's: -4.00 in S7;
- UltraFan WB: -3.38, with no Re-engine;
- the Trent 1000 upgrade beside the option: -5.98 when selected, beyond its $2B bill.
On its own, the upgrade earns nothing before 2042 without a Re-engine, so we hold it.

That's how you should hold it. That's our best view at the minute.
